Howdy, r.g.r.n. and n.a.o.!

If you're one of those who thinks Black Pudding Farming is cheating,
then don't read this.  I started this particular game *because* of all
the talk about pudding farming so that I could form my own opinions
about the practice.

The way I see it, yeah, pudding farming is a way to amass enormous piles
of loot.  Enormous, as in, hundreds of pages of #loot menus.  It's also
a way to accelerate the practice of altar camping.  What I mean by that
is, in real player time, it's much quicker to acheive the same goals
as any other altar camping (spawning, casting create monster, etc.).
With puddings, you don't have to be as patient.

Another couple of points.  By the time I was able to really even start the
farm, I was just as prepared for the planes as I was when I finally went.
I probably won't do this again, partly because I expect the next version
of the game to disallow it somehow, and partly because I see it as a
zero sum.  The effort balances the rewards to a certain extent,
although the kind of stuff you get might make Wishless easier.  But I
ended up using the stuff from the farm during the time I was farming, so
it's not like it was some especially huge advantage.  The protection
points were nice, but you can get those with create monster just as
effectively, and I lost the AC on the Plane of Fire anyway.  The HPs
were nice, but most of them came from b!oFH, not from Golden Glows. 

There are risks involved, some of them are not obvious, and some derive
from the boredom of the long middle game.  In the long run, the only thing
farming really did for my character is make the middle game much longer.
(You can't take the piles of loot with you to the planes.)

Like all my games, I played an Elven Ranger, starting as a pacifist, doing
the protection racket, and at the first mistake that breaks pacifism,
I play as a regular Elf Ranger, taking advantage of armor, weapons,
and spellcasting ability.  I play the same character so often, that it's
really hard to think of anything that was really difficult in this game.
Coaligned minetown, very forgettable bones piles, and and early magic
lamp (used throughout the game for light.)  This game had a Ft. Ludious,
the first one in a long run without the portal.

I had a black pudding farm on DL5, and I lived there for a long, long,
long, long time.  It was fun.

Oh yeah, the most fun part of the whole game, my PONY!  I had an awesome
Ki-Rin throughout most of the game, all the way to the Plane of Fire.

But it got boring, and I'm going to have to get back to work, so I
decided it was time.

Earth was easy, since I had plenty of digging wands and a c?oGD.
Plane of Air was the easiest thing in the world.  I just tamed all
the AE's.  My scrolls didn't make it though, so I had to find the portal
the hard way.

Fire, on the other hand, nearly ended the game several times, took FOREVER
to get out of, and THANKS to the folks on irc.freenode.org:/#nethack
who gave me the moral support I needed to do it.

First, something killed my Ki-Rin, I think somehow with boulders.  Note:
The +7 elven helm wasn't the best thing for him, I guess.  That caused me
to go burdened, since I lost my steed out from under me.  Poor Ki-Rin.
Right away, Rodney showed up, gated in Archons, and I was in trouble.
I started across the Plane hoping to find the portal quickly, but I
could not find it.  Then, malignant auras cursed my unicorn horn, and
unblessed my bag.  I dropped my bag.  Then got stunned.  And as soon
as I moved off my bag, a Fire Giant put boulders over it.  So there
I was, surrounded by Archons with all my stuff under a boulder that I
couldn't move.  I went berserk, and in the process of (nearly) extincting
Archons, Rodney took the Amulet, and I, being impatient and careless,
<ESC>'d past the messages.  So I survived this situation only to be in
a new predicament of having lost the Amulet!

The self-inflicted lava incidents were the only deaths in the game.

Well, I got my bag back, and almost immediately stepped in lava, the first
of two lava incidents.  Fortunately I had spare "AoLS.  I had another
battle almost exactly like the first one, but at least I never lost
control of my bag!  But I did step in lava one more time, being stupid,
zapping an empty wand and moving in the direction I wanted to zap...
So I had to drop the bag, freeze the lava, dig, and I *still* had to
find the stupid Amulet (again.)

Made it to Water.  Water was hard, even with sea creatures genocided.
Again, Rodney stole the amulet and I had to find it, and I could never
find the portal.  When I did find the portal, it was under a boulder.
Had to poly the boulder, and AGAIN realized I'd lost the Amulet.

Found it.  Got unburdened, and went to Astral.  Had to go to all three
altars.

I wanted to play around some more, but at one point I was stunned and
deathly sick, and rather than YASD on the High Altar of Mars with the
Amulet, reasonable thinking prevailed, and I went ahead and ascended.



